  "body": "The \"[check the usability of the image argument](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/canvas.html#check-the-usability-of-the-image-argument)\" steps for HTML/SVG images have the rule:\n\n> If image has a [natural width](https://drafts.csswg.org/css-images/#natural-width) or [natural height](https://drafts.csswg.org/css-images/#natural-height) (or both) equal to zero, then return bad.\n\nCSS allows for an image's natural dimensions to have a width and/or height that \"don't exist\"\n\n> The term [natural dimensions](https://drafts.csswg.org/css-images/#natural-dimensions) refers to the set of the natural height, natural width, and natural aspect ratio (the ratio between the width and height), each of which may or may **not exist** for a given [object](https://drafts.csswg.org/css-images/#objects).\n\nIs it the intention of this algorithm that images without a natural width and height (such as some SVG images) will not return bad? \n\nIf it is the intention, I would propose making it extra clear and noting that non-existent natural width/height are allowed.",
